// Openrunic relational data model.
//
// Conventions that hold for every model unless a doc comment says otherwise:
//
//   * `id String @id @db.Uuid` with NO database default. Ids are UUIDv7 values
//     minted in application code by `uuidv7()` from `@openrunic/database`. One
//     canonical identifier scheme everywhere: no serial columns, no dual keying,
//     no separate "public id" overlay. UUIDv7 is time-ordered, so primary-key
//     inserts stay append-friendly and `ORDER BY id` approximates insertion order.
//   * `tenantId String @db.Uuid` on every model except Organisation itself, with
//     a real foreign key to Organisation and `onDelete: Cascade` so per-tenant
//     purge is one delete. Every composite index and every unique constraint is
//     tenant-prefixed.
//   * `createdAt` / `updatedAt` on every model, including append-only ones.
//   * Money is integer cents (`Int`), never floats. Quantities that are
//     genuinely fractional (lab values, dispensed quantity, billed units) are
//     `Decimal` with an explicit precision.
//   * Clinical rows are never hard-deleted. Correction is a status transition
//     (`ENTERED_IN_ERROR`, `VOIDED`, `CANCELLED`) plus an AuditEvent.
//
// Coded data vs. enums (deliberate rule):
//
//   * A Prisma enum is used only where the value set is genuinely closed and
//     owned by us or by a frozen HL7/FHIR value set: workflow statuses,
//     lifecycle states, priorities, ranks.
//   * Anything drawn from a licensed or evolving terminology - ICD-10-CM, CPT,
//     HCPCS, LOINC, SNOMED CT, RxNorm, CVX, NDC, UCUM, X12 CARC/RARC - is a
//     plain `String` code plus a `codeSystem` string, optionally resolvable
//     against TerminologyCode. Terminology is bring-your-own and is never
//     vendored into this repository, so it must never be baked into the schema.
//
// Relations vs. provenance stamps (deliberate rule):
//
//   * A foreign-key relation is declared where the reference is part of the
//     record's meaning and is queried (patient, encounter, ordering provider,
//     assignee, claim line to charge item).
//   * "Who stamped this" provenance fields - `signedById`, `reviewedById`,
//     `postedById`, `publishedById`, `voidedById`, `completedById` - are plain
//     indexed `@db.Uuid` columns holding a User id without a declared relation.
//     They behave like AuditEvent.actorId: they must survive a user record
//     being deactivated, and they never drive a join in a hot path.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Multi-tenancy: three layers, only one of which is in this file
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Layer 1 (this file + `createTenantClient`): every tenant-scoped model carries
// `tenantId`, and `createTenantClient` injects it into every query and every
// write. The root `createPrismaClient` is still exported, because migrations,
// the seed and the CLI legitimately predate any tenant; request paths must not
// use it, and an ESLint boundary rule enforces that in `apps/api`.
//
// Layer 2 (Postgres RLS): ENFORCED as of migration
// `20260813120000_row_level_security`. Defence in depth for the case where
// layer 1 fails open - a bug in the extension, a raw query, a Prisma operation
// the extension does not yet know about. Every table below carries the policy;
// the migration file is the authority, and this is its shape:
//
//     ALTER TABLE "Patient" ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
//     ALTER TABLE "Patient" FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
//
//     CREATE POLICY "tenant_isolation" ON "Patient"
//       AS PERMISSIVE FOR ALL TO PUBLIC
//       USING      ("tenantId" = nullif(current_setting('openrunic.tenant_id', true), '')::uuid)
//       WITH CHECK ("tenantId" = nullif(current_setting('openrunic.tenant_id', true), '')::uuid);
//
// and on Organisation itself, which has no `tenantId` because it is the tenant:
//
//     CREATE POLICY "tenant_isolation" ON "Organisation"
//       AS PERMISSIVE FOR ALL TO PUBLIC
//       USING      ("id" = nullif(current_setting('openrunic.tenant_id', true), '')::uuid)
//       WITH CHECK ("id" = nullif(current_setting('openrunic.tenant_id', true), '')::uuid);
//
// What is enforced, and why each piece is there:
//   * FOR ALL: USING filters SELECT, UPDATE and DELETE, WITH CHECK filters the
//     rows INSERT and UPDATE may produce. One policy covers all four verbs, so
//     no verb can be the one somebody forgot.
//   * FORCE: without it the table owner is exempt from its own policies, and
//     the whole arrangement is theatre. FORCE applies to the owner too. It does
//     not defeat SUPERUSER or BYPASSRLS - nothing does - so the migration
//     refuses to grant privileges to an application role holding either.
//   * Fail closed: `current_setting(..., true)` returns NULL when the setting
//     was never set, and `"tenantId" = NULL` is NULL, so an undeclared session
//     reads zero rows rather than every row. The `nullif(..., '')` handles the
//     other case - a customized option that was set and then reset reads back
//     as the empty string, and `''::uuid` raises rather than returning nothing,
//     which would turn the first query on a recycled connection into an error.
//   * Transaction-scoped: `withTenantSession` (src/rls.ts) issues
//     `set_config('openrunic.tenant_id', $1, true)` as the first statement of
//     the transaction that will carry the query. Postgres discards a local
//     setting at COMMIT, so a pooled connection cannot leak one request's
//     organisation into the next. Nothing here uses a session-level SET, which
//     would have exactly that bug.
//   * Roles: migrations and the seed run as the OWNER; the application connects
//     as a separate non-owner, non-superuser role (`openrunic_app` by default)
//     holding only SELECT/INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE, no TRUNCATE, and no UPDATE or
//     DELETE on AuditEvent. Creating that role is an operator step - a password
//     cannot live in a migration - and is documented in README.md. BYPASSRLS is
//     never granted to anything.
//   * Consequence for future migrations: DDL is unaffected, but a data
//     statement run by the owner is filtered like anyone else's. A backfill
//     that has to span tenants must either loop with the setting applied per
//     organisation, or drop to `ALTER TABLE ... NO FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY`
//     and back inside the same transaction. See README.md.
//
// Layer 3 (tests): `src/rls.integration.test.ts` builds a throwaway database
// owned by a non-superuser role, replays this migration history into it and
// asserts denial as the application role - reads, updates, deletes and inserts
// across the boundary, the undeclared session, and FORCE against the owner.
// `apps/api` additionally attempts a cross-tenant read and write through every
// repository path.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// AuditEvent hash chain
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// AuditEvent is append-only and hash-chained per organisation. The chaining
// rule, implemented and unit-tested in `src/audit.ts`:
//
//   seq       - monotonic per tenant, starting at 1, no gaps.
//   prevHash  - the `hash` of the event with `seq - 1` for the same tenant, or
//               AUDIT_GENESIS_HASH (64 '0' characters) for seq 1.
//   hash      - sha256Hex(prevHash + "\n" + canonicalJson(chainedPayload)),
//               where chainedPayload holds the event's chained fields with
//               object keys sorted lexicographically at every depth and no
//               insignificant whitespace.
//
// Only the fields in `AUDIT_CHAINED_FIELDS` participate; `createdAt` and
// `updatedAt` deliberately do not, so a row rewritten by a backup restore with
// a different write timestamp still verifies. Any mutation or deletion of a
// past row breaks every subsequent hash, which the nightly verification job
// detects. `@@unique([tenantId, seq])` makes a duplicate sequence number
// impossible; `@@unique([tenantId, hash])` makes a replayed event impossible.

generator client {
  provider            = "prisma-client"
  output              = "../src/generated/prisma"
  moduleFormat        = "esm"
  importFileExtension = "js"
}

datasource db {
  provider = "postgresql"
}

