/// What a movement did to the shelf. Mirrors `MovementKind` in
/// `@openrunic/inventory` - all nine values, including the two transfer kinds no
/// endpoint produces yet - because the mirror is what `EnumParityProof` proves,
/// and a value present on one side only would make that proof decorative.
///
/// Direction is the kind's job and never the sign of the quantity, so a removal
/// cannot be entered as a receipt with a minus in front of it.
enum StockMovementKind {
  RECEIPT
  RETURN
  TRANSFER_IN
  COUNT_SURPLUS
  DISPENSE
  ADMINISTER
  WASTE
  TRANSFER_OUT
  COUNT_SHORTFALL
}

/// Where a lot stands. Mirrors `LotStatus` in `@openrunic/inventory`, which
/// fails closed on a value it does not recognise rather than falling through to
/// the expiry check. Recalled stock reading as available is the one failure in
/// that package that reaches a patient, so the column type is the cheapest
/// possible second copy of the guard.
enum StockLotStatus {
  AVAILABLE
  QUARANTINED
  RECALLED
  RETIRED
}

/// The business event a set of movements records.
///
/// Deliberately not the same set as StockMovementKind, and the difference is why
/// the posting table exists: one count produces surpluses on some lots and
/// shortfalls on others, and stock sent back to a supplier leaves as a
/// TRANSFER_OUT while tablets handed back by a patient arrive as a RETURN. The
/// movement says which way the stock went; this says what happened.
enum StockPostingKind {
  OPENING
  RECEIPT
  DISPENSE
  ADMINISTRATION
  WASTAGE
  COUNT
  CORRECTION
  SUPPLIER_RETURN
  PATIENT_RETURN
}

